examples.question_text:
Chords $AB$ and $CD$ intersect at $P$. Given $AP=16\text{ cm}$, $PB=6\text{ cm}$ and $CP=12\text{ cm}$, find $PD$. Diagram not to scale.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=5mm,y=5mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont},draw=black,text=black]
\coordinate (A) at (-5.5,0.154); \coordinate (B) at (5.5,0.154);
\coordinate (C) at (0.448,-5.484); \coordinate (D) at (3.868,3.913);
\coordinate (P) at (2.5,0.154);
\draw (0,0) circle (5.502);
\draw (A)--(B) (C)--(D);
\fill (P) circle (1.2pt);
\path (A) node[left] {$A$} (B) node[right] {$B$} (C) node[below] {$C$} (D) node[above] {$D$} (P) node[below right] {$P$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Two secants $PAB$ and $PCD$ meet at the external point $P$, with $A$ and $C$ the nearer intersections. Given $PA=8\text{ cm}$, $AB=10\text{ cm}$ and $PC=6\text{ cm}$, find (a) $PD$ and (b) $CD$. Diagram not to scale.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=5mm,y=5mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont},draw=black,text=black]
\coordinate (P) at (-6.5,-3.922); \coordinate (A) at (-2.5,-3.922); \coordinate (B) at (2.5,-3.922);
\coordinate (C) at (-4.202,-1.993); \coordinate (D) at (2.693,3.792);
\draw (0,0) circle (4.651);
\draw (P)--(B) (P)--(D);
\fill (A) circle (1.2pt); \fill (C) circle (1.2pt);
\path (P) node[below left] {$P$} (A) node[below] {$A$} (B) node[below right] {$B$} (C) node[above left] {$C$} (D) node[above right] {$D$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

$PC$ is tangent to the circle at $C$. The secant $PNF$ passes through the centre $O$, with $N$ nearer to $P$. Given $PC=12\text{ cm}$ and $PN=6\text{ cm}$, find (a) $PF$ and (b) the chord length $NF$. Diagram not to scale.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=8mm,y=8mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont},draw=black,text=black]
\coordinate (O) at (0,0); \coordinate (P) at (5,0); \coordinate (N) at (3,0); \coordinate (F) at (-3,0); \coordinate (C) at (1.8,2.4);
\draw (O) circle (3);
\draw (P)--(F) (P)--(C) (O)--(C);
\draw (1.65,2.2)--(1.85,2.05)--(2,2.25);
\fill (O) circle (1.2pt);
\path (O) node[below] {$O$} (P) node[right] {$P$} (N) node[below right] {$N$} (F) node[left] {$F$} (C) node[above] {$C$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Chords $AB$ and $CD$ intersect at $P$. Their intercepts are $AP=x\text{ cm}$, $PB=(x+4)\text{ cm}$, $CP=6\text{ cm}$ and $PD=8\text{ cm}$. Derive an equation satisfied by $x$; do not solve it. Diagram not to scale.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=5mm,y=5mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont},draw=black,text=black]
\coordinate (P) at (0,0); \coordinate (A) at (-5.2111,0); \coordinate (B) at (9.2111,0);
\coordinate (C) at (0,6); \coordinate (D) at (0,-8);
\draw (2,-1) circle (7.2801);
\draw (A)--(B) (C)--(D);
\fill (P) circle (1.2pt);
\path (A) node[left] {$A$} (B) node[right] {$B$} (C) node[above] {$C$} (D) node[below] {$D$} (P) node[below left] {$P$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Chords $EF$ and $GH$ intersect at $Q$. Prove that $\triangle EQH\sim\triangle GQF$, then show that $EQ\times QF=GQ\times QH$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=11mm,y=11mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont},draw=black,text=black]
\coordinate (E) at (-1.6,1.2); \coordinate (F) at (1.6,-1.2);
\coordinate (G) at (-1.2,-1.6); \coordinate (H) at (1.2,1.6); \coordinate (Q) at (0,0);
\draw (0,0) circle (2);
\draw (E)--(F) (G)--(H) (E)--(H) (G)--(F);
\fill (Q) circle (1.2pt);
\path (E) node[above left] {$E$} (F) node[below right] {$F$} (G) node[below left] {$G$} (H) node[above right] {$H$} (Q) node[below] {$Q$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

Secants $TEF$ and $THG$ meet at the external point $T$, with $E$ and $H$ the nearer intersections. Given $TE=TH$, explain why $EF=HG$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=9mm,y=9mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont},draw=black,text=black]
\coordinate (T) at (-4,0); \coordinate (E) at (-1.6,1.2); \coordinate (F) at (0,2);
\coordinate (H) at (-1.6,-1.2); \coordinate (G) at (0,-2);
\draw (0,0) circle (2);
\draw (T)--(F) (T)--(G);
\fill (E) circle (1.2pt); \fill (H) circle (1.2pt);
\draw (-2.855,0.47)--(-2.745,0.73) (-2.855,-0.47)--(-2.745,-0.73);
\path (T) node[left] {$T$} (E) node[above left] {$E$} (F) node[above] {$F$} (H) node[below left] {$H$} (G) node[below] {$G$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

A planet is modelled as a sphere of radius $R$. An observer's eyes are at $P$, height $h$ above the surface point $N$. The line $PC$ is tangent at the horizon point $C$, and $PNOF$ lies on a straight line through the centre $O$. The diagram shows a circular cross-section and is not to scale.

(a) Show that $d=PC$ satisfies $d^2=h(h+2R)$.
(b) Find $d$ to the nearest metre when $h=4\text{ m}$ and $R=6200\text{ km}$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=9mm,y=9mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont},draw=black,text=black]
\coordinate (O) at (0,0); \coordinate (P) at (0,3.125); \coordinate (N) at (0,2.5); \coordinate (F) at (0,-2.5); \coordinate (C) at (1.5,2);
\draw (O) circle (2.5);
\draw (P)--(F) (P)--(C) (O)--(C);
\draw (1.35,1.8)--(1.15,1.95)--(1.3,2.15);
\fill (O) circle (1.2pt);
\path (O) node[left] {$O$} (P) node[above] {$P$} (N) node[left] {$N$} (F) node[below] {$F$} (C) node[right] {$C$};
\path (0,2.8125) node[right] {$h$} (0.75,1) node[below right] {$R$} (0.75,2.5625) node[above right] {$d$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
